{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The 'negative justification' fails because Popper's own criterion for preferring one unfalsified theory over another relies on 'severity of tests,' which implicitly assumes that surviving severe tests is a reliable indicator of future performance—a form of induction. If the preference were truly based only on the elimination of rivals (as the target claims), then any unfalsified theory, regardless of how poorly tested, would be equally preferable to a severely tested one, provided no rivals exist. However, Popper explicitly argues we should prefer the better-tested theory. This preference cannot be derived from mere eliminative logic (which treats all non-falsified theories as equal survivors) but requires an inductive leap that past test survival correlates with future reliability, contradicting the target's claim that no positive inductive support is needed.","premise":"Popper prefers better-corroborated theories over less-tested unfalsified theories, not just any unfalsified theory.","premises_essential":["596171fca475e9f8ad46a117cf1455d9df3c0c86bdcda6be6d57099d1933f81f"],"successor_question":"How can Popper justify preferring a severely tested theory over an untested but unfalsified one without assuming that past test survival predicts future success?","target":"596171fca475e9f8ad46a117cf1455d9df3c0c86bdcda6be6d57099d1933f81f"}]}